// The list functional-update builtins: each returns a NEW list, never mutating
// in place (proposal-value-semantics-mvp §3.2). There is no `xs[i] := v` -- an
// "updated" list is a fresh list, and the original binding is unchanged.
//
// As with the iteration combinators, the higher-order forms take a thunked
// closure (`thunk { fn(x) { ... } }`): functions are computations, and only
// values pass as arguments. A bare list literal is check-only, so the base is
// ascribed (the list discipline of example 07).
def xs = ([10, 20, 30] : List(Integer));

// `set` replaces by index; `update_at` maps one element through a closure;
// `insert_at` and `remove_at` change length at an index; `push`, `append`,
// and `concat` grow by one, by another list, or by a list of lists. Each `xs`
// here is the SAME original -- none of these edits touch it.
def replaced = list.set(xs, 1, 99);
def bumped = list.update_at(xs, 0, thunk { fn(n) { n + 1 } });
def inserted = list.insert_at(xs, 1, 15);
def removed = list.remove_at(xs, 0);
def grown = list.push(xs, 40);
def joined = list.append(xs, [40, 50]);
def flattened = list.concat([xs, [40, 50]]);

// `update_where` maps only the elements a predicate accepts. Only 20 and 30
// exceed 15, so they double; 10 is kept unchanged.
list.update_where(thunk { fn(n) { n > 15 } }, thunk { fn(n) { n * 2 } }, xs)

// The harness checks (the update_where result):
//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-last-value: [10, 40, 60]
